    <h2>4. Your files and how we protect them</h2>
    <p>
        Protecting your data is the first principle Cmdr is built on. Here's what that means in practice, what we ask of
        you in return, and what happens when we get it wrong.
    </p>

    <h3>How Cmdr is built</h3>
    <p>
        Cmdr is built so that a crash, a full disk, or a cable pulled at the wrong moment is far less likely to cost you
        a file:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            When Cmdr replaces an existing file, it writes the new version to a temporary file alongside it and swaps it
            in only once every byte has landed, so an interrupted write doesn't leave a half-written file wearing your
            filename. Replacing a file with a folder, or a folder with a file, across two different devices is the one
            case Cmdr can't do this way, and it tells you before it starts.
        </li>
        <li>
            Deleting asks first, every time, and nothing in the app's own settings turns that off. The one exception is
            the MCP server described in section 5: if you turn it on and hand a client the access token, that client can
            delete without the dialog. Where the filesystem has a Trash, that's the default and the dialog says so. On a
            filesystem without one (network shares, FAT32 and exFAT, phones over USB, inside an archive) the delete is
            permanent, and the dialog warns you before it runs.
        </li>
        <li>
            Long-running file operations show honest progress, and you can pause or cancel them at any point, including
            after you've sent them to the background. Canceling stops the remaining work; it doesn't undo what already
            finished, and a delete can't be undone at all.
        </li>
        <li>
            The data-writing paths carry a large automated test suite, including deliberately hostile cases: a transfer
            killed partway through, a device disconnected mid-copy, a backend that fails on demand, and cancels and
            rollbacks at every stage. Free space is checked before an operation starts.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        This describes how we build Cmdr and how we intend to keep building it. Please hold us to it. It isn't a
        warranty that nothing will ever go wrong.
    </p>

    <h3>Please keep backups</h3>
    <p>
        Cmdr copies, moves, renames, and deletes real files on real disks, sometimes across a flaky network, on
        operating systems that change underneath it. No software is free of defects, so we can't guarantee the safety
        of your data.
    </p>
    <p>
        So please keep current backups of anything you'd be upset to lose, and check now and then that you can actually
        restore from them. <strong>Cmdr is a file manager, not a backup tool</strong>, and it isn't a substitute for
        one. Before something large or unusual (a bulk rename across thousands of files, a move onto a share you've
        just mounted, or the first run of a new feature on files you care about), take a moment to confirm your backup
        is current.
    </p>

    <h3>Reporting problems</h3>
    <p>
        If Cmdr loses or corrupts a file, we want to know, quickly and in detail. Write to
        <a href="mailto:hello@getcmdr.com">hello@getcmdr.com</a> with what you were doing and what happened, or use
